ROTINC
++++++

SUMMARY
-------

Rotate a set of 3 perpendicular components either around the vertical direction
(TO VRT or TO VNE) or around the T direction (TO LQT)

SYNTAX
------

ROTATIONS AROUND THE VERTICAL
.............................

  ``ROTINC {TO VRT|VNE} {VERBOSE}``

ROTATIONS AROUND THE TRANSVERSE (SH)
....................................

Assumes cmpaz = baz - 90 


  ``ROTINC {TO LQT} {INCIDENCE angle} {VERBOSE}``

or

  ``ROTINC {TO LQT} {iP | iS} {vP alpha} {vS beta} {RAY} {VERBOSE}``


INPUT
-----
   :TO VRT: rotate into vertical,radial, transverse coordinate system

   :TO VNE: rotate into system aligned with vertical, north, and east

   :TO LQT: rotate into P, SV, and SH coordinate system

   :INCIDENCE a: Rotation will be around T with output L cmpinc the angle a

   :iP: incident P wave: L cmpinc is apparent angle

   :iS: Incident S wave: Q cmpinc is apparent angle (

   :vP v: v is P-wave velocity in surface layer (default 5.8)

   :vS v: v is S-wave velocity in surface layer (default 3.36)

   :RAY v: ray parameter (s/km), sin(theta)/V where theta is the incident angle

   :VERBOSE: prints information to the screen (default off)

DESCRIPTION
-----------

Given three perpendicular components of a recorded time series, ROTINC rotates
the set into the selected coordinate system. It works its way through all
records in memory until it fails to find three consecutive perpendicular
traces. It assumes vertical up (cmpinc = 0.0), cmpaz measured clockwise from N.
T to the right from the vertical-radial plane facing towards the station(baz -
90).  Hence, VNE, VRT, and LQT are all left-handed coordinate systems.

For TO LQT, the "TO LQT" can be left out because that is the only option for
which there are additional arguments.  For TO LQT there are two choices:

  1) ROTINC INCIDENCE angle is a rotation around the T axis (angle=0.0 is up)
  2) ROTINC iP or iS is a rotation around T with a calculated angle

The calculated angle assumes the P or S wave is incident on the free
surface (top of a half space).  For iP, the output cmpinc for L is often called
the apparent angle: the arctangent of the radial (R) amplitude divided by the
vertical (V) amplitude.  For iS, the output cmpinc for Q the S-wave apparent
angle minus 90 degrees (because SV has the opposite polarity on V and R). For TO
LQT iS, the S-wave incident angle must be less than the critical angle: vP * RAY
less than 1.0.

EXAMPLES
--------

Rotations around the vertical are straightforward: either TO VNE or TO VRT with no
arguments.

For a whole-space rotation tO LQT::

  SAC> rotinc incidence 24.44 verbose
     Target Coordinate System: LQT

For a free-surface correction to LQT where RAY = sin(21.32)/5.8) = 0.063::

  SAC> ROTINC iP RAY 0.063 VERBOSE
    Target Coordinate System: LQT
    Apparent Angle Method: 'vP Free Surface'
    vP: 5.80 km/s vS: 3.36 km/s Ray Param: 0.063000 s/km
    Apparent angle: 24.44
  SAC> evaluate &1,cmpinc
    24.4416
  SAC> message (&1,kcmpnm)
    L

Generally vP and vS are the velocities in the surface layer.  If the wavelength
of the dominant arrivals are larger than the thickness of that layer, one may
have to average over two or more layers.  This can be tested by plotting the
vertical-radial particle motion (PLOTPM_).  The ray parameter will not be
changed.  Here is an example for the same ray but assuming the wavelengths are
long enough to average over the whole crust::

  SAC> ROTINC iP vp 8.04 vs 4.47 RAY 0.063 VERBOSE
    Target Coordinate System: LQT
    Apparent Angle Method: 'vP Free Surface'
    vP: 8.04 km/s vS: 4.47 km/s Ray Param: 0.063000 s/km
    Apparent angle: 32.71

Here is an example for iS (RAY = sin(23.12)/3.36)::

  SAC> ROTINC iS RAY 0.1169 VERBOSE
    Target Coordinate System: LQT
    Apparent Angle Method: 'Vsv Free Surface'
    Vp: 5.80 km/s Vs: 3.36 km/s Ray Param: 0.116900 s/km
    Apparent angle: 64.18
  SAC> evaluate &2,cmpinc
    64.183
  SAC> message (&2,kcmpnm)
    Q

There is often interference with other arrivals for SV on the vertical, so the
SV on V and R are generally not that similar.  Hence V-R particle motion is less
useful for SV than it is for P.

EQUATIONS
---------

The relevant cmpinc (apparent angle) is computed using the following equations:

  * iP:    L cmpinc = atan(2*Vs*p*sqrt(1-(Vs*p)**2)/(1-2*(Vs*p)**2) )

  * iS:    Q cmpinc = atan(Vp*(1-2*(Vs*p)**2))/(2*Vs**2*p*sqrt(1-(Vp*p)**2)) )

where p is the ray parameter. Equations are based on Problem 5.6 in
Aki and Richards, 2002, pg. 184.

